{:edb/db-before-t 2592 :edb/db-after-t 2593 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535905 50 #inst "2026-10-09T10:23:30.387Z" 13194139535905 true] [17592186124522 1020 #edb/ref 17592186373741 13194139535905 true] [17592186124523 1020 #edb/ref 17592186373734 13194139535905 true] [17592186124946 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535905 true] [17592186124946 1100 "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & a \\\\ 3 & b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$ find the value of $\\frac{b}{a}.$" 13194139535905 true] [17592186124946 1100 "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 6\\end{bmatrix}, D = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}, P = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & a \\\\ 3 & b\\end{bmatrix}$\nGiven that $D = P^{-1}AP$, find the value of $\\frac{b}{a}$." 13194139535905 false] [17592186124946 1145 "From the diagonal matrix $D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$\n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$ Computing $A-3I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 6\\end{aligned}]-(3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{2}{1}=2.$" 13194139535905 true] [17592186124947 1017 #edb/ref 17592186122704 13194139535905 false] [17592186124947 1017 #edb/ref 17592186373748 13194139535905 true] [17592186124947 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535905 true] [17592186124947 1100 "Given the matrix\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$,\nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D = P^{-1}AP$." 13194139535905 false] [17592186124947 1100 "Given the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$, \nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}AP.$" 13194139535905 true] [17592186124947 1145 "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix. Since matrix $A$ is triangular, its eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=3$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $2$nd one). Thus, ${x}_{1}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=0.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a homogeneous system of linear equations with the matrix as above. Note, the matrix is already in row echelon form. There is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535905 true] [17592186373734 1017 #edb/ref 17592186373735 13194139535905 true] [17592186373734 1089 "q-140038" 13194139535905 true] [17592186373734 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535905 true] [17592186373734 1100 "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}11 & -8 \\\\ 12 & -9\\end{aligned}].$ You're given that ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ are the eigenvalues of $A$, and\n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$, ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ are the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=A,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix." 13194139535905 true] [17592186373734 1113 #uuid "152de614-310b-5169-9d3a-b26f9bc07c9a" 13194139535905 true] [17592186373734 1145 "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535905 true] [17592186373735 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373736 13194139535905 true] [17592186373735 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373737 13194139535905 true] [17592186373735 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373738 13194139535905 true] [17592186373735 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373739 13194139535905 true] [17592186373735 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373740 13194139535905 true] [17592186373735 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535905 true] [17592186373735 1041 "selection" 13194139535905 true] [17592186373735 1104 #edb/ref 17592186373739 13194139535905 true] [17592186373735 1110 #uuid "1d1135ba-2178-5e51-adef-36de78675659" 13194139535905 true] [17592186373736 1034 "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" 13194139535905 true] [17592186373736 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535905 true] [17592186373736 1111 #uuid "961bae0e-7819-512a-89ab-e9168684277f" 13194139535905 true] [17592186373737 1034 "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" 13194139535905 true] [17592186373737 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535905 true] [17592186373737 1111 #uuid "324f9ee4-75b5-55db-a322-0eba9f63ca87" 13194139535905 true] [17592186373738 1034 "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}2 